//! # `vos-probe-ready`: the fixture of the VOS-028b capacity probe
//!
//! The probe (`code/programs/rust/vos-capacity-probe`, experiment E1) measures how fast a
//! *generated by-value harness* enumerates claims.  The real scheduler `vos-sched-fp-bitmap`
//! is fixed at `N = 8`, so the sizes the probe needs beyond that (VOS30 section 9.7: the
//! `insert` claim at `N = 10` with 262,144 cases and a binder product of 393,216, just under
//! the 400,000 budget) come from this crate.
//!
//! ## The ready set, as a bitmap
//!
//! A set of thread ids `0..N` is one machine word with bit `i` set when `i` is ready:
//!
//! ```text
//!   mask = 0b0000_0110      threads 1 and 2 are ready
//!   insert8(mask, 3)     -> 0b0000_1110
//!   insert8(mask, 9)     -> 0b0000_0110   (9 >= N: nothing changes)
//! ```
//!
//! `insert8` and `pick8` work on a `u8` (N = 8); `insert10` works on a `u16` of which only
//! the low ten bits mean anything (N = 10).  Every function is total: an id past `N`
//! changes nothing, and the unused high bits of a `u16` are never set.
//!
//! This is a class H fixture, not a verified-OS component: it has no contract, no shape rules
//! and nothing depends on it except the probe.

#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

/// Insert thread `t` into an eight-thread ready set; ids `>= 8` change nothing.
pub fn insert8(mask: u8, t: u8) -> u8 {
    if t >= 8 {
        mask
    } else {
        mask | (1u8 << t)
    }
}

/// Insert thread `t` into a ten-thread ready set; ids `>= 10` change nothing.
pub fn insert10(mask: u16, t: u8) -> u16 {
    if t >= 10 {
        mask
    } else {
        mask | (1u16 << t)
    }
}

/// The highest ready thread of an eight-thread set, or 255 for the empty set.
///
/// The highest set bit is `7 - leading_zeros`; zero has no set bit.
pub fn pick8(mask: u8) -> u8 {
    if mask == 0 {
        255
    } else {
        (7 - mask.leading_zeros()) as u8
    }
}
